/**
  @dir Rodin/Variational
  @brief Namespace @ref Rodin::Variational
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Variational
  @brief Module which provides the necessary tools for constructing and
  solving variational problems.

  The Variational module is the core of the Rodin finite element framework.
  It provides classes for defining finite element spaces, trial and test
  functions, variational forms, boundary conditions, and problems.

  ## Finite Element Spaces

  - @ref Rodin::Variational::P0 "P0" — Piecewise constant (discontinuous)
    finite element space
  - @ref Rodin::Variational::P0g "P0g" — P0 with gradient enrichment for DG methods
  - @ref Rodin::Variational::P1 "P1" — Piecewise linear (continuous)
    finite element space
  - @ref Rodin::Variational::H1 "H1" — Higher-order @f$ H^1 @f$-conforming
    finite element space (arbitrary polynomial order)

  ## Functions

  - @ref Rodin::Variational::TrialFunction "TrialFunction" — Represents the
    unknown solution @f$ u @f$ in a variational formulation
  - @ref Rodin::Variational::TestFunction "TestFunction" — Represents the
    weighting function @f$ v @f$
  - @ref Rodin::Variational::GridFunction "GridFunction" — A concrete function
    storing DOF values in a finite element space

  ## Variational Forms

  The module supports building variational problems of the form:
  @f[
    \text{Find} \quad u \in U \quad \text{ s.t. } \quad \forall v \in V \quad
    a(u, v) = b(v) \ ,
  @f]
  which are translated into linear algebra problems @f$ \mathbf{A}\mathbf{x}
  = \mathbf{b} @f$.

  Key expression classes:
  - `Integral(A, B)` — Domain integrals (bilinear or linear forms)
  - `BoundaryIntegral(A, B)` — Boundary integrals
  - `FaceIntegral(A, B)` — Integrals over all faces (interior + boundary)
  - `InterfaceIntegral(A, B)` — Integrals over interior faces only

  Differential operators:
  - `Grad(u)` — Gradient operator
  - `Div(u)` — Divergence operator
  - `Jacobian(u)` — Jacobian (gradient of vector field)
  - `Transpose(A)` — Matrix transpose

  Algebraic operators:
  - `Dot(A, B)` — Inner product / Frobenius contraction
  - `Trace(A)` — Matrix trace
  - `Frobenius(A)` — Frobenius norm
  - `Component(u, i)` — Extract @f$ i @f$-th component of vector function

  DG operators:
  - `Jump(u)` — Jump across internal faces: @f$ [u] = u^+ - u^- @f$
  - `Average(u)` — Average across internal faces: @f$ \{u\} = (u^+ + u^-)/2 @f$

  Boundary conditions:
  - `DirichletBC(u, g)` — Essential boundary conditions
  - `PeriodicBC(u, dofMap)` — Periodic boundary conditions

  Convenience functions:
  - `RealFunction` — Scalar function from constant or lambda
  - `VectorFunction` — Vector function from initializer list
  - `Zero` — Zero scalar/vector function
  - `Pow`, `Exp`, `Sqrt`, `Sin`, `Cos` — Pointwise math operations

  ## Problem

  The @ref Rodin::Variational::Problem "Problem" class assembles all forms
  and boundary conditions into a solvable linear system.

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 Vh(mesh);
  TrialFunction u(Vh);
  TestFunction  v(Vh);

  Problem poisson(u, v);
  poisson = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v))
          - Integral(f, v)
          + DirichletBC(u, Zero());

  Solver::CG(poisson).solve();
  @endcode

  @see @ref guides-variational "Variational Formulations Guide"
 */

/**
  @dir Rodin/Distance
  @brief Namespace @ref Rodin::Distance
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Distance
  @brief Module which provides models for computation of the distance function.

  The Distance module offers several methods for computing distance functions
  on finite element meshes, each with different trade-offs between accuracy,
  speed, and smoothness.

  ## Available Methods

  | Method | Class | Equation | Properties |
  |--------|-------|----------|------------|
  | **Eikonal** | `Distance::Eikonal` | @f$ \|\nabla d\| = 1 @f$ | Exact distances via FMM; supports signed distance with interior/interface regions |
  | **Poisson** | `Distance::Poisson` | @f$ -\Delta u = 1, \; u\|_{\partial\Omega} = 0 @f$ | Smooth approximation; fast (one linear solve); does not give exact distances |
  | **SignedPoisson** | `Distance::SignedPoisson` | Signed version of Poisson | Combines Poisson solves for interior/exterior, yielding a signed smooth distance |
  | **Rvachev** | `Distance::Rvachev` | R-function composition | Constructs distance-like fields from Boolean operations on primitives |
  | **SpaldingTucker** | `Distance::SpaldingTucker` | Spalding-Tucker approximation | Boundary-layer approximation for wall-distance computations |

  All methods inherit from a common CRTP `Base<Derived>` that provides
  `setInterior()` and `setInterface()` methods for specifying the signed-distance
  regions.

  ## Typical Usage

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 Vh(mesh);
  GridFunction d(Vh);

  // Eikonal (exact, uses FMM)
  Distance::Eikonal eikonal(d);
  eikonal.setInterface(interfaceAttr)
         .setInterior(interiorAttr)
         .solve()
         .sign();

  // Poisson (smooth approximation, one linear solve)
  Distance::Poisson poissonDist;
  auto u = poissonDist(Vh);
  @endcode

  @see @ref Rodin::Eikonal "Eikonal module" for the underlying FMM solver.
 */

/**
  @dir Rodin/Hilbert
  @brief Namespace @ref Rodin::Hilbert
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Hilbert
  @brief Module which provides models and tools for the Hilbertian
  extension-regularization procedure.

  The Hilbert module implements @f$ H^1 @f$-based extension-regularization
  operators used in shape and topology optimization. The primary class
  `H1a` solves the regularized extension problem:
  @f[
    \alpha^2 \int_\Omega \nabla u : \nabla v \, dx + \int_\Omega u \cdot v \, dx = \ell(v)
  @f]
  where @f$ \alpha @f$ is a regularization length-scale parameter that controls
  the smoothness of the extended field.

  This is used, for example, to extend and regularize a shape derivative
  (defined only on the boundary) to a smooth descent direction in the full
  computational domain.
 */

/**
  @dir Rodin/Advection
  @brief Namespace @ref Rodin::Advection
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Advection
  @brief Module which provides models for the resolution of unsteady advection
  equations.

  The Advection module implements semi-Lagrangian (characteristic-based)
  transport of scalar fields on unstructured meshes. The primary class is
  @ref Rodin::Advection::Lagrangian "Lagrangian", which solves the
  advection equation:
  @f[
    \frac{\partial \phi}{\partial t} + \mathbf{v} \cdot \nabla \phi = 0
  @f]

  ## Algorithm

  At each time step, the Lagrangian method:
  1. **Traces characteristics** backward from each mesh vertex along the
     velocity field @f$ \mathbf{v} @f$ for a duration @f$ \Delta t @f$
  2. **Interpolates** the solution at the departure points
  3. **Projects** the interpolated values back onto the finite element space
     via an @f$ L^2 @f$ projection (mass-matrix solve)

  The characteristic tracing uses configurable time-stepping schemes. The
  default is Runge-Kutta 4 (`Math::RungeKutta::RK4`). A boundary policy
  (`TaylorBoundaryShiftPolicy`) handles characteristics that hit the domain
  boundary by clamping the footpoint inside with an inward nudge.

  ## Typical Usage

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 Vh(mesh);
  TrialFunction u(Vh);
  TestFunction  v(Vh);

  GridFunction phi(Vh);
  // ... initialize phi ...

  VectorFunction velocity{1.0, 0.0};  // uniform horizontal advection

  Advection::Lagrangian advection(u, v, phi, velocity);

  Real dt = 0.01;
  for (int step = 0; step < 100; ++step)
    advection.step(dt);
  @endcode

  @note The module is header-only (`Rodin::Advection` INTERFACE library).

  @see @ref Rodin::Variational::Flow "Flow" for the characteristic tracer.
 */

/**
  @dir Rodin/Eikonal
  @brief Namespace @ref Rodin::Eikonal
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Eikonal
  @brief Module which provides solvers for the Eikonal equation.

  The Eikonal equation is a non-linear partial differential equation of the form:
  @f[
    |\nabla u| = F(x)
  @f]
  where @f$ F(x) @f$ is a speed function. When @f$ F = 1 @f$, the solution
  represents the distance function.

  ## Fast Marching Method (FMM)

  The primary solver is @ref Rodin::Eikonal::FMM "FMM", which implements the
  Fast Marching Method on unstructured simplicial meshes. The algorithm
  propagates a wavefront from seed vertices, updating arrival times in
  causal order using a priority queue. Vertex states during propagation:

  | State | Description |
  |-------|-------------|
  | **Far** | Not yet visited |
  | **Considered** | In the priority queue, tentative value computed |
  | **Accepted** | Finalized — arrival time is correct |

  The FMM specialization for P1 finite elements on local meshes supports:
  - Arbitrary spatial dimension (2D triangular, 3D tetrahedral, surface meshes)
  - User-defined speed functions @f$ F(x) @f$
  - Seed interface specification (initial zero-distance vertices)

  ## Typical Usage

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 Vh(mesh);
  GridFunction u(Vh);

  // Speed function F(x) = 1 (distance computation)
  auto speed = [](const Geometry::Point&) -> Real { return 1.0; };

  Eikonal::FMM fmm(u, speed);
  fmm.seed(interfaceVertices).solve();
  @endcode

  @note The module is header-only.

  @see @ref Rodin::Distance "Distance module" for higher-level distance
  function computation that wraps FMM.
 */

/**
  @dir Rodin/Elasticity
  @brief Namespace @ref Rodin::Elasticity
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Elasticity
  @brief Module which provides models for solid mechanics and elasticity.

  This module implements the linearized elasticity bilinear form for small
  deformations. The primary class is
  @ref Rodin::Variational::LinearElasticityIntegrator "LinearElasticityIntegral",
  which assembles:
  @f[
    a(\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}) = \int_\Omega \bigl[\lambda (\nabla \cdot \mathbf{u})(\nabla \cdot \mathbf{v})
    + 2\mu \, \boldsymbol{\varepsilon}(\mathbf{u}) : \boldsymbol{\varepsilon}(\mathbf{v})\bigr] \, dx
  @f]
  where @f$ \boldsymbol{\varepsilon}(\mathbf{u}) = \tfrac{1}{2}(\nabla \mathbf{u} + (\nabla \mathbf{u})^T) @f$
  is the symmetric strain tensor, and @f$ \lambda, \mu @f$ are the Lamé
  parameters.

  ## Typical Usage

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 Vh(mesh, mesh.getSpaceDimension()); // vector P1 space
  TrialFunction u(Vh);
  TestFunction  v(Vh);

  Real lambda = 0.5769, mu = 0.3846;
  VectorFunction f{0, -1};

  Problem elasticity(u, v);
  elasticity = LinearElasticityIntegral(u, v)(lambda, mu)
             - BoundaryIntegral(f, v).over(GammaN)
             + DirichletBC(u, VectorFunction{0, 0}).on(GammaD);

  Solver::CG(elasticity).solve();
  @endcode

  @see @ref examples-pdes-elasticity "Elasticity example"
  @see @ref Rodin::Solid "Solid module" for hyperelastic (large deformation) models
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Solid
  @brief Hyperelastic solid mechanics module for large-deformation problems.

  The Solid module provides a layered framework for finite-strain
  hyperelasticity. It is organized into four sub-layers:

  ## Kinematics

  - `KinematicState` — Stores the deformation gradient @f$ \mathbf{F} @f$,
    Jacobian @f$ J = \det\mathbf{F} @f$, and right Cauchy-Green tensor
    @f$ \mathbf{C} = \mathbf{F}^T\mathbf{F} @f$ at a quadrature point.
  - `IsotropicInvariants` — Computes principal invariants @f$ I_1, I_2, I_3 @f$
    from @f$ \mathbf{C} @f$.

  ## Constitutive Laws

  Each law derives from `HyperElasticLaw<Derived>` (CRTP base) and provides:
  - A `Cache` struct for precomputed data
  - `setCache(cache, cp)` — populate the cache from a `ConstitutivePoint`
  - `getFirstPiolaKirchhoffStress(P, cache, cp)` — @f$ \mathbf{P} = \partial W / \partial \mathbf{F} @f$
  - `getMaterialTangent(dP, cache, cp, dF)` — @f$ D\mathbf{P}[\delta\mathbf{F}] @f$

  | Law | Energy density @f$ W @f$ |
  |-----|--------------------------|
  | `Hooke` | @f$ \lambda(\operatorname{tr}\boldsymbol{\varepsilon})\mathbf{I} + 2\mu\boldsymbol{\varepsilon} @f$ (linear, small strain) |
  | `NeoHookean` | @f$ \frac{\mu}{2}(I_1 - d) - \mu\ln J + \frac{\lambda}{2}(\ln J)^2 @f$ |
  | `SaintVenantKirchhoff` | @f$ \frac{\lambda}{2}(\operatorname{tr}\mathbf{E})^2 + \mu\,\mathbf{E}:\mathbf{E} @f$ |
  | `MooneyRivlin` | @f$ c_1(\bar{I}_1 - d) + c_2(\bar{I}_2 - d) + \frac{\kappa}{2}(J-1)^2 @f$ |

  ## Integrators

  - `InternalForce<Law, FES>` — Nonlinear residual (internal force vector):
    @f$ R_i = \int_\Omega \mathbf{P} : \nabla\phi_i \, dX @f$
  - `MaterialTangent<Law, Solution, FES>` — Tangent matrix:
    @f$ K_{ij} = \int_\Omega D\mathbf{P}[\nabla\phi_j] : \nabla\phi_i \, dX @f$
  - `BodyForce<FES>` — External body-force vector
  - `TractionForce<FES>` — Boundary traction vector

  ## Derived Fields

  - `FirstPiolaKirchhoffStress` — Post-process @f$ \mathbf{P} @f$ field
  - `CauchyStress` — Post-process @f$ \boldsymbol{\sigma} = J^{-1}\mathbf{P}\mathbf{F}^T @f$
  - `GreenLagrangeStrain` — Post-process @f$ \mathbf{E} = \frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{C} - \mathbf{I}) @f$

  ## Typical Usage

  A transient hyperelastic cantilever beam using `NeoHookean`:

  @code{.cpp}
  #include <Rodin/Solid.h>

  P1 Vh(mesh, mesh.getSpaceDimension()); // vector P1 space
  TrialFunction u(Vh);
  TestFunction  v(Vh);

  Solid::NeoHookean law(lambda, mu);

  // Nonlinear residual and tangent
  auto R = Solid::InternalForce(law, v, u.getSolution());
  auto K = Solid::MaterialTangent(law, u, v, u.getSolution());
  @endcode

  @note The module is header-only (`Rodin::Solid` INTERFACE library).

  @see @ref Rodin::Elasticity "Linear elasticity" for small-deformation problems.
 */

/**
  @dir Rodin/QF
  @brief Namespace @ref Rodin::QF
 */

/**
  * @namespace Rodin::QF
  * @brief Module for defining quadrature formulae over reference polytopes.

  * The QF (Quadrature Formula) module provides numerical integration rules
  * for computing integrals over reference polytopes. These quadrature rules
  * approximate integrals by weighted sums of function evaluations:
  * @f[
  *   \int_{\hat{K}} f(\hat{x}) \, d\hat{x} \approx \sum_{i=1}^n w_i \, f(\hat{x}_i)
  * @f]
  * where @f$ \hat{K} @f$ is a reference polytope, @f$ \hat{x}_i @f$ are the
  * quadrature points, and @f$ w_i @f$ are the associated weights.
  *
  * ## Available Quadrature Rules
  *
  * | Rule | Class | Polytopes | Exactness |
  * |------|-------|-----------|-----------|
  * | **Centroid** | `QF::Centroid` | All types | Degree 1 (linear). Single point at barycenter. |
  * | **Gauss-Legendre** | `QF::GaussLegendre` | All types | Degree @f$ 2n-1 @f$ for @f$ n @f$ points (1D). Tensor-product or Duffy transform for higher dimensions. |
  * | **Gauss-Lobatto** | `QF::GaussLobatto` | Segments | Degree @f$ 2n-3 @f$ for @f$ n @f$ points. Includes endpoints (useful for spectral methods). |
  * | **Grundmann-Möller** | `QF::GrundmannMoller` | Simplices only | Degree @f$ 2s+1 @f$ for parameter @f$ s \geq 0 @f$. Designed specifically for simplicial elements. |
  *
  * ## Automatic Selection
  *
  * During assembly, the quadrature order is automatically chosen based on the
  * polynomial degree of the finite element space and the integrands. Users can
  * also specify a quadrature rule explicitly:
  *
  * @code{.cpp}
  * QF::GaussLegendre qf(Polytope::Type::Triangle, 5); // order 5 on triangles
  * @endcode
  *
  * ## Higher-Dimensional Construction
  *
  * - **Tensor-product geometries** (quadrilaterals, hexahedra): constructed via
  *   tensor products of 1D Gauss-Legendre rules.
  * - **Simplicial geometries** (triangles, tetrahedra): the Duffy transformation
  *   maps tensor-product rules to the simplex, or Grundmann-Möller rules are
  *   used natively.
  *
  * Rodin provides quadrature rules for all supported polytope types
  * (segments, triangles, quadrilaterals, tetrahedra, hexahedra, wedges).
 */

/**
  @dir Rodin/FormLanguage
  @brief Namespace @ref Rodin::FormLanguage
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::FormLanguage
  @brief Module for writing high level abstract expressions.

  The FormLanguage module provides the expression template infrastructure
  underlying Rodin's variational form language. It enables composing
  mathematical expressions that are lazily evaluated during assembly.

  ## Key Classes

  - `FormLanguage::Base` — Root base class for all form language objects.
    Provides unique identification (UUID), object lifetime management via
    shared ownership, and polymorphic `copy()`. **Not thread-safe**: use
    `thread_local` caching for per-thread output in evaluation methods.

  - `FormLanguage::Traits<T>` — Type trait template. Each form language type
    provides a specialization defining associated types (`ScalarType`,
    `RangeType`, `FESType`, etc.). Always specialize `Traits` for new types.

  - `FormLanguage::List` — Container for variational form components (integrals,
    boundary conditions) that makes up the body of a `Problem`.

  ## Expression Template Pattern

  Operators like `Grad`, `Div`, `Dot` are CRTP-based expression templates:

  @code{.cpp}
  auto stiffness = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v)); // expression, not yet evaluated
  auto load = Integral(f, v);                   // another expression
  Problem p(u, v);
  p = stiffness - load + DirichletBC(u, Zero()); // combined expression
  @endcode

  Expressions store operands via `std::reference_wrapper` (non-owning) or
  `std::unique_ptr` (owning, for temporaries). No computation occurs until
  assembly iterates over mesh cells and evaluates the expression at quadrature
  points.

  ## Object Lifetime

  The `Base::object()` helpers manage temporary object lifetimes. When an
  expression captures a temporary (e.g.\ a lambda-constructed `RealFunction`),
  the temporary is stored in a `shared_ptr` table within the expression,
  ensuring it lives as long as the expression does.

  @see @ref Rodin::Variational "Variational module" for the user-facing form language.
 */

/**
  @dir Rodin/Math
  @brief Namespace @ref Rodin::Math
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Math
  @brief Module for general mathematical operations.

  Provides mathematical types, constants, and utility functions used
  throughout the Rodin framework.

  ## Types

  | Type | Description |
  |------|-------------|
  | `Math::Vector<T>` | Dense column vector (Eigen-based) |
  | `Math::Matrix<T>` | Dense matrix (Eigen-based) |
  | `Math::SparseMatrix<T>` | Compressed sparse column matrix |
  | `Math::SpatialVector<T>` | Small fixed-size vector for physical coordinates |
  | `Math::SpatialMatrix<T>` | Small fixed-size matrix (e.g.\ Jacobians, stress tensors) |
  | `Math::LinearSystem` | Couples operator matrix @f$ A @f$, RHS vector @f$ b @f$, and solution @f$ x @f$ |

  ## Constants

  `Math::Constants::pi()` — @f$ \pi @f$

  ## Mathematical Functions

  | Function | Signature | Domain |
  |----------|-----------|--------|
  | `abs` | `abs(x)` | Scalar or matrix |
  | `exp` | `exp(x)` | Scalar or matrix |
  | `sqrt` | `sqrt(x)` | Scalar or matrix |
  | `pow` | `pow(base, exp)` | Scalar or matrix |
  | `pow2` | `pow2(x)` | Scalar or matrix |
  | `log` | `log(x)` | Scalar |
  | `log2` | `log2(x)` | Scalar |
  | `log10` | `log10(x)` | Scalar |
  | `sin`, `cos`, `tan` | Trigonometric | Scalar or matrix |
  | `asin`, `acos`, `atan` | Inverse trig | Scalar |
  | `atan2` | `atan2(y, x)` | Two-argument arctangent |
  | `sinh`, `cosh`, `tanh` | Hyperbolic | Scalar or matrix |
  | `min`, `max` | Element-wise | Scalar |
  | `clamp` | `clamp(x, lo, hi)` | Scalar |

  ## Unit Types

  - `Math::Rad` — Radian wrapper with implicit `Real` conversion
  - `Math::Deg` — Degree wrapper with conversion to `Rad`

  ## Runge-Kutta Schemes

  - `Math::RungeKutta::RK2` — Second-order Runge-Kutta (midpoint)
  - `Math::RungeKutta::RK4` — Classical fourth-order Runge-Kutta

  ## Root Finding

  - `Math::RootFinding::Bisection` — Bisection method for scalar root finding

  ## Linear Algebra Utilities

  - `dot(a, b)` — Inner product
  - `cross(a, b)` — Cross product (3D)
  - `sum`, `minus`, `mult`, `division` — Element-wise operations
  - `conj` — Complex conjugate
  - `nan<T>()` — NaN sentinel
 */


/**
  @dir Rodin/Solver
  @brief Namespace @ref Rodin::Solver
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Solver
  @brief Module for solving linear and nonlinear systems arising from
  finite element discretizations.

  The Solver module provides a comprehensive set of direct and iterative
  linear solvers:

  **Direct solvers** (exact up to floating-point precision):
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::SparseLU "SparseLU" — General-purpose sparse LU factorization
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::UMFPack "UMFPack" — SuiteSparse UMFPACK solver
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::CHOLMOD "CHOLMOD" — SuiteSparse Cholesky for SPD systems
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::SimplicialLLT "SimplicialLLT" — Simplicial @f$ LL^T @f$
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::SimplicialLDLT "SimplicialLDLT" — Simplicial @f$ LDL^T @f$
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::SparseQR "SparseQR" — Sparse QR factorization
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::SPQR "SPQR" — SuiteSparse sparse QR
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::HouseholderQR "HouseholderQR" — Dense Householder QR
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::LDLT "LDLT" — Dense @f$ LDL^T @f$

  **Iterative solvers** (for large systems):
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::CG "CG" — Conjugate Gradient (SPD systems)
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::BiCGSTAB "BiCGSTAB" — Bi-conjugate gradient stabilized
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::GMRES "GMRES" — Generalized Minimum Residual
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::DGMRES "DGMRES" — Deflated GMRES
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::MINRES "MINRES" — Minimum Residual (symmetric systems)
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::IDRS "IDRS" — Induced Dimension Reduction
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::IDRSTABL "IDRSTABL" — IDR(s) with stabilization
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::LeastSquaresCG "LeastSquaresCG" — Least-squares CG

  Usage:
  @code{.cpp}
  // Inline iterative solver — most common pattern
  Solver::CG(problem).solve();

  // Configured iterative solver
  Solver::CG solver(problem);
  solver.setMaxIterations(1000);
  solver.setTolerance(1e-10);
  solver.solve();

  // Direct solver
  Solver::SparseLU(problem).solve();
  @endcode

  **Nonlinear solvers** (for hyperelastic or other nonlinear problems):
  - `Solver::NewtonSolver<LinearSolver>` — Newton-Raphson iteration for nonlinear
    variational problems. At each iterate @f$ u^k @f$, assembles the tangent
    system @f$ J(u^k)\,\delta u^k = -F(u^k) @f$ and updates
    @f$ u^{k+1} = u^k + \delta u^k @f$. Configurable via `setMaxIterations()`,
    `setAbsoluteTolerance()`, and `setRelativeTolerance()`.

  @see @ref guides-solvers "Solvers Guide"
 */

